From: damnbitimcrit <333091414+damnbitimcrit@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Halo 3] Draw cut ticks as is; smooth the first-person eye,
 melee and hand

Four things only showed with the frame rate unlocked:

- Cutscene cuts: a shot change happens in one tick (the script sets the
  new shot, restarts every actor's animation without interpolation and
  re-places it; then the objects update). The camera hook showed the new
  shot from the first frame, but the objects had already been blended
  from the old shot's last state, so the actors slid into place over the
  tick. Now the tick that cuts is found when it ends, from the scripted
  camera's globals (another mode, a new camera point set without a move,
  another camera animation or shot, the same shot set again, the
  cinematic's own shot; not a camera transition) and is drawn as is:
  objects and first-person poses. The camera hook uses the same decision
  (the globals' new-command byte alone no longer cuts: the Cortana effect
  sets it too); a cut it finds only by a jump draws the rest of the tick
  as is (the exact state is put back before the observer and the frame's
  publication). After several ticks in one frame, the scripted camera
  keeps blending from an estimate of where it was a tick ago instead of
  cutting.
- Objects the game moves without blending in a tick (a unit's custom
  animation started without interpolation, 823910A4; a scenery animation
  started, 8236139C; object_teleport / object_set_facing, 822BB210;
  ai_teleport and cs_teleport, 82509E84, 82509FD4, 825137D0) are drawn as
  is for that tick, with what is attached to them. An object is only
  blended over the tick's own state, and in the main menu, where the UI
  turns the player model every frame outside the ticks, objects placed
  between ticks are drawn as is at the next tick.
- First-person legs: looking down, the eye moves toward the head by an
  offset in the frame of the unit's aiming vector (+0x1A8) and the
  biped's eye offset (+0x4B4), both per tick, around the blended body;
  while turning the view smoothly, the eye hopped around the body at
  30 Hz and the nearby legs with it. Both are blended with the biped
  (restored on their own: a later change keeps the object blended).
- First-person melee and grenade throws: any one of the pose's matrices
  turning more than 60 degrees in a tick dropped the blend of the whole
  pose for that tick, and these are the fastest animations, while the
  game blends almost every first-person animation change itself. Now a
  pose (still as a whole: its matrices are absolute) is drawn as is only
  on an identity change, a matrix moving over 0.5 units or turning over
  150 degrees, or over 60 degrees in a tick its animation state (P+0x30)
  changed in. halo3_fp_snap = any also snaps on any turn over 60 degrees
  in a tick; off keeps only identity changes. The hand IK weight
  (B+0x23B4, which pulls the left hand onto the weapon at render time
  and fades out and in around melee and throws) is blended too, while
  the weapon pose is. A melee lunge's turn toward its target, which
  player_control takes over in one frame after each tick
  (82215090-82215118), is drawn spread over the tick (the drawn facing
  only; the live facing and the simulation are untouched).

Stats: cuts by source, objects drawn as is on cuts and their largest
move, objects moved unblended by the game, objects placed between ticks;
first-person pose changes, ticks drawn as is, ticks turning over 60
degrees and the largest turn, facings taken over by the game and the
frames that drew them spread.
